id="definition">Definition</string><string id="acc_definition">definition</string><string id="acc_resources">resources</string><string id="acc_search_input">search</string><string id="acc_pause">pause</string><string id="acc_play">play</string><string id="acc_replay">replay</string><string id="acc_submit">submit</string><string id="acc_next">next</string><string id="acc_previous">previous</string><string id="acc_volume">volume</string></string_table></string_tables><sounds enabled="false" /><nav_data><outline><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw" displaytext="Three-Phase Power" expand="true"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6OmRQ6TeVQY" displaytext="Three-Phase Power" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6IEWHluuIxN" displaytext="What is a &quot;Phase&quot;?" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6B45KaYmNf0" displaytext="Single-Phase Power" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6LHSpWo7Se3" displaytext="Three-Phase Power" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6SaLV04Gm0D" displaytext="Three-Phase Distribution Transformer" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6r1wEv0xBav" displaytext="Three-Phase Sine Wave - Animated" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.5uLKiaCfKTb" displaytext="Balanced Load" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.66fG2jkKvqn" displaytext="Three-Phase AC Generator - Animated" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.66nVYowbpM0" displaytext="Three-Phase Wye Circuit" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6YpN5LeStBR" displaytext="Three-Phase Delta Circuit" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6aWI1gjGPPv" displaytext="Wye vs. Delta" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6ToLUOBpigz" displaytext="The Ground Wire" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.5vgtVnuJKMH" displaytext="Sources" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6aYliiy9Piw.6lc3Q9ALbG8" displaytext="CC BY 3.0" expand="true" /></links></slidelink></links></outline><search><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6OmRQ6TeVQY" slidebank="false">three-phase power </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6IEWHluuIxN" slidebank="false">what is a "phase"? the relationship in time between the successive states or cycles of an oscillating or repeating system such as an alternating electric current. [1] an electrical phase travels in a sine wave pattern a complete cycle is 360° </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6B45KaYmNf0" slidebank="false">single-phase power used for smaller loads like lighting and heating. in the us, 120v is the standard single phase voltage. standard frequency is usually either 50hz or 60hz. cannot be used in generators. would result in catastrophic failure due to the way the load is handled in a single phase. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6LHSpWo7Se3" slidebank="false">three-phase power click image to enlarge a type of polyphase system. most common method used to transfer power around the world. deliver power over three hot wires where the voltage across any two hot wires measures 208vac. each line is 120° in phase. at any given time, power is being generated on at least two lines.  click image to close </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6SaLV04Gm0D" slidebank="false">three-phase distribution transformer three-phase transformer with four wire output for 208y/120 volt service: one wire for neutral, others for a, b and c phases. three-phase distribution transformer is licensed cc by sa by glogger </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6r1wEv0xBav" slidebank="false">three-phase sine wave - animated </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.5uLKiaCfKTb" slidebank="false">balanced load a load is considered balanced when each phase has an equal amount of current. unbalanced loads could lead to a drop in voltage on the line, tripped circuit breakers, or blown fuses, and has an increased impact as it travels down the line. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.66fG2jkKvqn" slidebank="false">three-phase ac generator - animated </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.66nVYowbpM0" slidebank="false">three-phase wye circuit click image to enlarge also called "star" configuration. can be a 3-wire or 4-wire configuration. (the neutral wire, is optional.) joins one end of each of the coils and applies the individual phases to the open ends. can run delta devices if the neutral is omitted.   click image to close </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6YpN5LeStBR" slidebank="false">three-phase delta circuit click image to enlarge delta configurations require only 3 wires. used in power transmission due to the expense of running the fourth, neutral wire. used for any large motors or heaters that don’t need a neutral.  click image to close </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6aWI1gjGPPv" slidebank="false">wye vs. delta delta configuration has higher reliability. providing they can carry the load, the remaining  windings can produce full voltage if one fails. wye configuration offers more flexibility. can provide both 120vac and 208vac based on need.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.6ToLUOBpigz" slidebank="false">the ground wire the 3-wire or 4-wire designation does not count the ground wire. the ground wire does not carry a load under normal circumstances and is only used for fault protection.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6aYliiy9Piw.5vgtVnuJKMH" slidebank="false">sources [1] dictionary.com, 'the definition of phase', 2015. [online]. available: http://dictionary.reference.com/browse/phase.  </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>